+# Disconnected Adaptive Comfort mode model
+
+Experimental MIT contribution candidate, not a live Climate Advisor mode. The implemented module `custom_components/climate_advisor/adaptive_mode_preview.py` models selection, holds and logical hand-back for one zone. It must not import the existing engine, band calculator, timer resolver, HA, storage, clock or any command service. No production path imports it.
+
+## Assumptions and boundaries
+
+- Selecting Adaptive Comfort is an overlay on the ordinary policy, not destructive editing of its settings. A policy evaluation request identifies which policy to evaluate using **current** settings and evaluation time; it contains no captured target.
+- All requests/decisions permanently expose `commands_allowed=False`. They are not authorization tokens, dispatch proposals or proof of equipment safety. This model does not execute the selected policy, alter host automation flags or change a thermostat.
+- A caller supplies immutable host snapshots and explicit synthetic validity/approval evidence. `ready` is an input to this model, not an implementation of sensor, unit, equipment or safety validation. Device-specific P-series/M10 tests remain pending.
+- Each reconstructed per-zone lifecycle needs a fresh caller-supplied session ID, not shared with another zone. Generations increase on transitions that invalidate work. The caller must retain the returned state; using an obsolete state instead of current state is not supported. Nothing is persisted or automatically resumed here.
+- Host `owner_revision` advances for every manual target/mode/owner-selection change, including changes back to the same value. `settings_revision` advances on ordinary-policy edits; `routine_revision` advances on adaptive configuration edits. These are explicit fake-host assumptions, not existing Climate Advisor APIs or evidence that real HA events can be correlated reliably.
+- Ordinary settings edited while adaptive is selected are not rolled back. Such an edit invalidates old evaluation requests, but new hand-back requests reference current settings. A changed owner or ordinary policy selection instead establishes a manual/external hold.
+- When the overlay owns no selection, hold or pending return, it delegates ordinary evaluation without imposing its hold policy. Rejected enable during an existing host hold leaves ordinary behavior intact; an ordinary evaluation request is not permission to bypass the host's own guards.
+- No timeout, timer expiry, repeated enable, normal schedule refresh or recovered sensor can clear a hold latched by the adaptive lifecycle. Explicit resume requires reconciliation and valid current evidence; an ongoing external hold still blocks it.
+
+## Data and API
+
+- `HostPolicy`: ordinary `policy_id`, owner/settings revisions, and retained `automation_enabled=False`, `observe_only=True`, `external_hold=False` defaults. The reserved adaptive policy ID cannot stand in for the ordinary return policy.
+- `ModeInputs`: host snapshot, adaptive routine revision, `approved=False` and `ready=False` by default.
+- `ModeState`: required session ID; initially unselected, generation zero, no saved policy, hold or hand-back. Saved policy information is only a return reference; its old settings/permissions are never written back.
+- `ModeEvent`: event kind, originating session/generation, optional explicit reconciliation and matching hand-back request. Unknown/malformed/stale events are withheld.
+- `decide_mode(state, inputs, event)`: returns an immutable next state, reason/explanation and optional evaluation request. Invalid internal state raises `ValueError` instead of inventing a reset state; malformed live inputs invalidate work and latch reconciliation for an owned lifecycle. Explicit disable still deselects adaptive even when inputs are malformed; unresolved hand-back stays unknown, or a known manual hold is preserved.
+- `request_is_current(request, state, inputs)`: checks a request against the current session, generation, full input snapshot, selected policy and holds. This is a pure stale-work check, not atomic HA dispatch or a substitute for command expiry/safety checks. Requests do not cache a temperature or evaluation time; evaluate the named policy afresh when consuming one.
+
+## Transition contract
+
+| Event | Model behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| `refresh` while never selected | Request the current ordinary policy unchanged; do not mutate settings or host permissions. |
+| `enable` | Require approval/readiness and no hold or pending hand-back. Save the ordinary policy reference once, select adaptive and invalidate earlier requests. Repeated valid enable is idempotent. |
+| `refresh` while selected | Request only adaptive evaluation; ordinary schedule/offset changes cannot become a second selected producer in this model. A changed routine revision, lost approval or invalid required input latches reconciliation. |
+| `manual_change` or changed ownership/ordinary policy while selected or returning | Latch manual hold and invalidate outstanding requests. No counter-request. Pending/unknown hand-back is abandoned in favor of the newer authority, not reported successful. |
+| `resume` | Require explicit reconciliation and absence of an external hold. If adaptive remains selected, validate approval/readiness and bind to the current ordinary policy/owner and routine revision. If disabled, return only to the current ordinary policy. Never enable host automation. |
+| `disable` while still owned and valid | Deselect adaptive first, invalidate earlier work, then request evaluation of the saved ordinary policy using its current snapshot. Mark logical hand-back pending, not completed. |
+| `disable` while held or superseded | Deselect without any restore request. Preserve a manual hold/newer authority; if reconciliation is needed instead, retain an unknown hand-back. Repeated disable cannot clear it. |
+| `handback_confirmed` | Only the matching current pending request can finish logical hand-back. No physical restoration is inferred. |
+| `handback_failed` | Only the matching request can mark the outcome unknown; no automatic retry or ordinary/adaptive fall-through. |
+| `retry_handback` | An unknown result may be retried only after explicit reconciliation, current readiness and unchanged ownership. Issue a new generation-bound request; never blindly replay the old one. |
+| `restart` during adaptive selection, hold or hand-back | Invalidate work and latch reconciliation (preserve an existing manual hold). Pending hand-back becomes unknown. A later refresh cannot resume automatically. No host restart code is changed. |
+| `manual_change` / `restart` / `resume` when the overlay owns nothing | Invalidate selector metadata, but leave the ordinary host's hold/startup policy alone; no new adaptive hold or settings change. |
+
+Input loss during a pending hand-back also makes it unknown. Changed ownership during hand-back retains the newer choice and blocks stale confirmation. An ordinary settings revision change rejects an old confirmation; it does not allow applying captured settings. Explicit reconciliation is required before a fresh hand-back request.
+
+## Test scope and remaining work
+
+The 101 cases in `tests/test_adaptive_mode_preview.py` exercise the real decision function, immutable fake host snapshots, independent ordinary/adaptive evaluators, and matched/mismatched outcome events. They cover current-time/current-settings evaluation on exit, no double producer in the selector, preserved disabled/observe-only permissions, stale session/generation/context rejection, manual/restart persistence and unknown outcomes without retry. The suite includes all 216 three-event sequences from six lifecycle event kinds, checking invariants and old-request rejection.
+
+Tests were written before the module and first failed at import. Additional review tests then exposed off-mode hold interference and incomplete disable behavior with invalid inputs; those failures were reproduced before correcting the model. All 101 cases now pass together with 147 band/timing cases and 196 existing regressions (444 total) on Python 3.14.5 / Windows. Scoped Ruff lint/format checks pass. The existing full-suite Windows `resource` blocker remains deferred, not skipped or patched; the HA tier was not run.
+
+Using the isolated unit tier described in [DEVELOPMENT.md](DEVELOPMENT.md):
+
+```sh
+python scripts/test.py unit tests/test_adaptive_mode_preview.py tests/test_sleep_timing.py tests/test_sleep_trajectory.py
+```
+
+This establishes only the synthetic decision portion of M01–M08 and outcome bookkeeping for M09. It does not establish real callback coverage, ownership detection, physical release, sensor freshness, quantization, command rate limits, HA atomicity, or any live M10/P-series requirement. Before a production selector, enumerate the host's classification, bedtime/wake, restore, savings/pre-cool, occupancy/away/vacation, ventilation, chart and remote-sensor consumers and agree feature precedence with maintainers. In particular, this model does not rewrite the host's deliberate manual-grace or startup policies.
+
+The ordinary host behavior remains unchanged because nothing imports this module. The browser demo remains independent. No Home Assistant installation, network access, hardware, dependency addition or golden-scenario change is needed for this slice.
