+# Disconnected sleep/wake trajectory preview
+
+**Local experimental slice, not wired into Climate Advisor.** The module `custom_components/climate_advisor/sleep_trajectory.py` calculates a proposed comfort band for one explicitly dated routine. No live planner, sensor, UI, chart, service call or persistent state imports it. Existing control behavior and configuration are unchanged; this is not a released feature or a sleep/comfort validation result.
+
+## Contract
+
+`preview_trajectory(config, *, now)` accepts a `SleepTrajectory` and an injected aware datetime. Configuration is immutable and disabled/unapproved by default. Enabling it does not imply approval. All temperatures use internal Fahrenheit; callers must not pass Celsius values without converting them at a separately validated boundary.
+
+An enabled, approved routine requires:
+
+- A `TrajectoryLimits` with explicit finite minimum/maximum temperatures, positive minimum band width and positive maximum per-edge rate in degrees Fahrenheit per hour. There are no household numeric defaults.
+- A tuple of exactly five `TrajectoryAnchor` records, with phases in this order: `wind_down`, `sleeping`, `waking`, `return_to_day`, `daytime`.
+- Strictly increasing, timezone-aware, dated anchor instants. These must already be resolved by the caller. The evaluator normalizes them to UTC for comparisons and elapsed arithmetic; it does not resolve ambiguous/nonexistent local clock times, repeat a schedule, infer a timezone or use the current clock.
+- Finite ordered floor/ceiling values at every anchor, inside the approved bounds and at least the declared minimum width apart.
+
+Both edges interpolate linearly. Wind-down may only decrease or hold; the sleeping interval holds both edges; waking may only increase or hold. The return-to-day interval may move in either direction within the same bounds/rate limits. The phase beginning at an anchor owns that instant. The final daytime anchor is included, but no values are extrapolated beyond the dated horizon.
+
+All anchors are checked before returning any preview, including anchors after the evaluation time. Invalid input is not clamped, reordered or repaired. Arithmetic uses rational values derived from the declared decimal numbers and exact elapsed microseconds, avoiding overflow in differences between extreme finite edges and preserving inclusive rate/width boundaries. Output floats are checked again; if representation would collapse the permitted band, the preview is withheld.
+
+## Fixed-wake local-time resolver
+
+`custom_components/climate_advisor/sleep_timing.py` now provides `LocalSleepTiming` and `resolve_sleep_timing(config, *, wake_date)`. It is also disconnected: no existing production module imports it. It resolves **one supplied date**, not a running recurring schedule, and has no temperature, approval, storage or command policy.
+
+All timing fields are explicit, with no defaults:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `timezone_key` | IANA timezone key; never inferred from the host |
+| `wind_down_start`, `sleep_start`, `wake_time` | Naive `datetime.time` values with `fold=0`; the resolver owns ambiguity policy |
+| `wind_down_day_offset`, `sleep_day_offset` | Exact integer -1 (previous date) or 0 (wake date); no inferred overnight wrap |
+| `warming_duration`, `return_duration` | Positive elapsed `timedelta` values |
+| Injected `wake_date` | A `date`, not a datetime or a clock read; the resolved wake starts `return_to_day` |
+
+Resolve wind-down, sleep start and wake into UTC first. Then subtract warming duration from wake and add return duration to wake in UTC. This preserves elapsed ramp lengths across clock changes. All five anchors must be strictly increasing; no sorting or compressing invalid intervals.
+
+- A repeated local time uses its earlier UTC occurrence, with an explanatory adjustment.
+- A nonexistent local time shifts forward by the actual offset gap, including non-hour gaps, with an explanation.
+- If that adjustment changes the requested calendar date (such as a skipped whole date), withhold instead of silently moving the routine to another date.
+- Missing/invalid timezone data, invalid dates/times/offsets/durations, overflow and overlapping phases return `withheld` with no partial anchors.
+
+`SleepTimingResolution` contains status, reason, explanation, immutable `TimingAnchor(at, phase)` records, adjustment messages and non-initializable `commands_allowed=False`. A resolved result does not approve or enable anything. Compose it with explicit bands/limits/flags and call `preview_trajectory()`; the combined tests show a correctly ordered spring-forward routine still being withheld when its shortened wind-down exceeds the rate limit.
+
+Timezone data comes from Python's `zoneinfo`: system IANA data or its `tzdata` fallback. The existing locked Windows unit environment uses `tzdata` 2026.6; no new dependency was installed. Tests do not skip missing-zone cases; runtime resolution explains unavailable data without downloading it. Timezone-data versions are part of reproducibility.
+
+There is no current-night selection, adjacent-date overlap resolution, scheduled callback, daylight input, cache invalidation or command replay prevention here. A future adapter must resolve again when the timezone/configuration/date changes and independently invalidate outstanding proposals. Repeated-time arithmetic tests are not proof of a scheduler firing only once.
+
+## Results and boundaries
+
+`TrajectoryPreview` has `status`, `reason_code`, `explanation`, optional `floor_f`, `ceiling_f` and `phase`, and a non-initializable `commands_allowed=False` field.
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `disabled` | Feature off; no band, no schedule evaluation required |
+| `withheld` | No approved/valid preview, or evaluation outside the dated horizon; no band |
+| `preview` | Valid mathematical band and phase, not a thermostat command or an achieved indoor condition |
+
+Withheld reasons distinguish unapproved/invalid configuration, invalid limits, anchors, times or bands, wrong phase direction, excessive rate, out-of-horizon evaluation, and unrepresentable output. Neither a valid result nor the boolean field is an authorization mechanism for a future dispatcher. This module has no such dispatcher.
+
+Not implemented: device-step quantization, choosing/changing HVAC mode, sensor freshness, manual override or restart reconciliation, a recurring schedule executor, daylight, weather offsets, thermal response, or control. Those require separate policy/integration work. In particular, a higher cooling setpoint can permit passive warming; it does not authorize heating or prove a room has warmed.
+
+## Test fixture and validation
+
+The tests use an arbitrary 60–80°F envelope, a 4°F minimum band width and a 2.5°F/hour per-edge maximum. The overnight floor/ceiling anchors are 68/74 → 64/70 → 64/70 → 69/75 → 68/74°F at 22:00, 00:00, 05:00, 07:00 and 09:00 UTC. These values are arithmetic examples, not recommended temperatures or equipment-safety defaults.
+
+Using the upstream unit-test environment described in [DEVELOPMENT.md](DEVELOPMENT.md):
+
+```sh
+python scripts/test.py unit tests/test_sleep_timing.py tests/test_sleep_trajectory.py
+```
+
+The tests cover off/unapproved behavior, anchors and intermediate points, minute-by-minute bounds/rates, deterministic sampling, UTC/offset equivalence, already-resolved DST gap/fold instants, malformed inputs, phase directions, extreme finite inputs and float representation. A structural test checks standard-library-only imports and that no other integration module references the calculator.
+
+The current checkpoint was tested on Python 3.14.5 / Windows with 70 timing cases, 77 band-preview cases, 101 separate [mode-model cases](adaptive-mode-preview.md) and 196 existing band/target/gate/restore/startup/observe-only cases passing together (444 total). The mode model is not a live consumer of these calculators. The timing tests were written first and initially failed because the new module did not exist. They cover overnight/year/leap-date boundaries, gap/fold resolution, non-hour gaps, elapsed ramps, skipped dates, invalid inputs, immutability, and composition without bypassing approval or slope limits. Ruff check and format checks pass for the new Python files. A pre-change full unit run was blocked during collection by existing imports of Unix-only `resource` in `tools/sim_harness/replay.py`, reached by `tests/test_nightly_ledger.py` and `tests/test_sim_generator.py`. That limitation was not patched or hidden by changing the suite. The real-HA tier and live equipment were not run.
